    "case_summary": "A 67-year-old male patient with uncontrolled diabetes was admitted to hospital on January 1st, 2023, with shortness of breath and hypoxia. The patient experienced COVID-19 infection at the end of December 2022. On January 11th, the patient suddenly developed new symptoms including nasal ache, dyspnea, and facial and nasal swelling.\n\nInitial examination showed:- Temperature: 36.5°C- Heart rate: 116- Respiratory rate: 26- Blood pressure: 150/88 mmHg- O2 saturation: 90% in room airLater examination revealed:- Facial and nasal swelling- Black eschar on the nasal root\n\nLaboratory Tests:- White blood cell count: 2 × 10^9/L- Creatinine: 178 umol/L- Blood sugar: 11.6 mmol/L- Hemoglobin A1C: 10.1%- Platelet count: 17 × 10^9/L- Albumin: 20.5 g/L- Ferritin: >2000 ng/mlImaging Studies:1. CT scan of headTitle: Head CT findingsDescription: Revealed pansinusitis along the maxillary, ethmoidal, and sphenoid sinuses2. CT scan of lungsTitle: Chest CT findingsDescription: Showed bilateral lung infiltrations3. Histopathological examinationTitle: Nasal mucosa histological examinationDescription: Haematoxylin and eosin staining showed presence of large amount of fungal hyphae and spores4. Blood metagenomics next-generation sequencing (mNGS):- Detected 22 sequence reads of Rhizopus microsporum (0.16% of all reads)- Detected 19 reads of Mucor racemosus (0.13% of all reads)",
